$schema: https://usm.dev/schema/v1.json
$id: smith-gray/secret-injection
$type: feature
$version: 1
$last_updated: "2026-09-22"
summary: |
  Injection is how a resolved secret reaches a consumer without passing through model context. The
  MVP supports four methods — environment variable, 0600 temp file, Unix socket/pipe, and exec
  wrapper — chosen by what the consuming process needs. The agent receives only a success code, a
  placeholder name, or a path it cannot usefully read.
$system: smith-gray/system
$service: smith-gray/sidecar
status: in-progress
intent: |
  A resolved secret is useless unless the process that needs it can actually get it. Most such
  processes are CLIs and services that expect an env var or a file path, not an in-process API — and
  returning the value to the agent would defeat the design. Injection bridges that gap while keeping
  the value on the non-LLM side of the boundary.
flows:
  - id: inject-env
    name: Inject as an environment variable
    description: For a child process that already reads env — CLIs and SDKs.
    steps:
      - id: s1
        action: receive
        target: Sidecar receives a resolve request scoped to inject-env for a named variable.
      - id: s2
        action: setup
        target: Resolve and decrypt the object.
      - id: s3
        action: send
        target: >-
          Spawn the consumer with the value in its environment; do not inherit it in the agent's
          env.
      - id: s4
        action: observe
        target: Return a success code only.
  - id: inject-file
    name: Inject as a 0600 temp file
    description: For tools that require a file — kubeconfig, certificates.
    steps:
      - id: s1
        action: receive
        target: Sidecar receives a resolve request scoped to inject-file.
      - id: s2
        action: setup
        target: Write the value to a temporary file owned by the consumer with mode 0600.
      - id: s3
        action: send
        target: Return the path to the consumer.
      - id: s4
        action: delete
        target: Remove the file when the consumer exits.
  - id: inject-socket
    name: Inject over a Unix socket or pipe
    description: For long-running local services.
    steps:
      - id: s1
        action: receive
        target: Sidecar receives a resolve request scoped for a socket or pipe consumer.
      - id: s2
        action: send
        target: Deliver the value over the established local channel.
      - id: s3
        action: delete
        target: Zeroise after delivery.
  - id: inject-exec
    name: Inject via the exec wrapper
    description: Run a command with secrets injected and the environment scrubbed.
    steps:
      - id: s1
        action: receive
        target: Sidecar receives safekeys exec --token ... -- <command>.
      - id: s2
        action: resolve
        target: Resolve all tokens required by the command.
      - id: s3
        action: setup
        target: >-
          Spawn the command as a child of the sidecar with a minimal environment and injected
          values.
      - id: s4
        action: observe
        target: Return the command's exit code; values never reach the caller.
contracts:
  - id: prefer-exec-and-env
    description: Exec-wrapper and env injection are preferred over returning values to a caller.
    must_have:
      - Documentation steers integrators to exec-wrapper and inject-env first
      - Returning resolved values on an agent's stdout is not a supported mode
      - A placeholder name or path is returned in place of a value
  - id: value-scoped-to-consumer
    description: An injected value is visible only to the intended consuming process.
    must_have:
      - Temp files are mode 0600 and owned by the consumer
      - Env injection targets the child process, not the agent's own environment
      - The agent's process cannot read the injected value back
  - id: injection-is-cleaned-up
    description: Transient injection artefacts do not outlive the consumer.
    must_have:
      - Temp files are removed when the consumer exits
      - Plaintext buffers are zeroised after delivery
      - No injection artefact is left in a world-readable location
tests:
  - id: env-injection-not-visible-to-agent
    flow: inject-env
    setup:
      injection_method: inject-env
      consumer: printenv MY_TOKEN
    expect:
      - assertion: the child process sees the value
      - assertion: the agent's own environment does not contain the value
      - assertion: the sidecar response contains no value
    contracts:
      - value-scoped-to-consumer
      - prefer-exec-and-env
  - id: temp-file-permissions
    flow: inject-file
    setup:
      injection_method: inject-file
    expect:
      - assertion: the temp file is mode 0600
      - assertion: the file is owned by the consumer, not the agent
      - assertion: the file is removed after the consumer exits
    contracts:
      - value-scoped-to-consumer
      - injection-is-cleaned-up
  - id: exec-returns-only-exit-code
    flow: inject-exec
    setup:
      command: sh -c 'test -n "$API_KEY"'
    expect:
      - assertion: the caller receives an exit code, not the value
      - assertion: the command's stdout is not augmented with the secret
    contracts:
      - prefer-exec-and-env
decisions:
  - id: four-methods-not-one
    decision: Support env, 0600 temp file, socket/pipe, and exec rather than a single injection mode.
    rationale: >-
      Consumers differ — some read env, some need a file path, some are long-running services.
      Forcing one mode would push integrators to work around the tool and handle plaintext
      themselves, which is the failure mode being designed out.
    status: accepted
  - id: exec-wrapper-preferred
    decision: Make the exec wrapper the preferred and best-documented path.
    rationale: >-
      It gives the strongest guarantee — the value exists only in the child's environment for the
      life of the command — and keeps the caller's return surface to an exit code.
    status: accepted
  - id: placeholder-over-value
    decision: Return a placeholder name or path instead of a value whenever a return is needed.
    rationale: >-
      It preserves the ergonomics of "here is something to use" without giving the agent anything
      sensitive.
    status: accepted
  - id: mvp-scope-socket-pipe-deferred
    decision: Ship env, file, and exec injection in the MVP; defer the long-running socket/pipe method.
    rationale: >-
      The three shipped methods cover every CLI and service consumer the two-agent demo exercises.
      The socket/pipe method exists for long-running local services, which is not an MVP shape, and
      the injector explicitly refuses the method rather than silently falling back to another — so a
      caller cannot be misled about which channel was used.
    consequences: >-
      inject: socket injection is not implemented in the MVP. The method enum is closed and the
      injector errors on it.
    status: accepted
    date: "2026-09-22"
implementation:
  primary: pkg/inject/inject.go
  test_code: pkg/inject/inject_test.go
  test_code_status: manual
see_also:
  - smith-gray/sidecar-resolution
  - smith-gray/exec-wrapper
